#N canvas 20 20 1900 1400 12;
#X text 20 20 m_launchpad -- the Launchpad Pro MK3 mapping layer \, and the only file allowed to talk to the device. It owns Programmer Mode \, the safe exit and (from step 4) the layout \, so that everything which can change what the surface IS lives in one place. Pads and buttons come out as named controls on param and disp \, and it knows NOTHING about what any of them does -- same layer \, same rules and the same silence about meaning as m_nano., f 58;
#X text 20 190 creation arg 1: the Pd channel that the Launchpad's OWN channel 1 lands on. REQUIRED. It is 1 on the Organelle -- Pd input slot 1 \, per .pdsettings -- and whatever slot the Launchpad fills on the Mac. inlet: a bang from u_init when the boot sequence reaches the Launchpad stage. outlet 1: surface ownership -- 1 while the device is in Programmer Mode and its LEDs are ours to light \, 0 the moment safe-exit hands it back. Wired to g_grid in u_root., f 58;
#X text 900 170 THE SAME ARGUMENT IS USED AS THE LIGHTING CHANNEL \, and that is an assumption worth knowing. The Launchpad's three animation modes are MIDI channels 1 \, 2 and 3 RELATIVE to its own port \, so static is the block \, flashing the block plus one and pulsing the block plus two. That only lines up when Pd's input slot and output port for this device are the SAME number -- which is why ref-midi.md says keep the Launchpad as device 1., f 58;
#X obj 20 520 inlet;
#N canvas 40 40 1400 760 init 0;
#X text 20 20 Launchpad startup \, and it is now two messages: enter Programmer Mode \, then raise ownership. THE GRID CLEAR IS GONE FROM HERE. LED state survives the mode switch so a clear is still mandatory -- but g_grid paints all 99 indices on its first frame after ownership goes up \, and that frame IS the clear. One writer for the LEDs \, and no second blanking step to keep in step with it., f 58;
#X text 500 20 LIFTED FROM u_init IN PHASE 6 \, where it lived because Phase 2 needed it and this file did not exist. What came across was the mode SysEx and the port pattern \; what did not is the 89-note until loop \, which was writing to a surface this file does not own., f 58;
#X obj 20 260 inlet;
#X obj 20 320 t b b;
#X msg 20 400 240 \, 0 \, 32 \, 41 \, 2 \, 14 \, 14 \, 1 \, 247;
#X obj 20 480 midiout;
#X obj 700 260 loadbang;
#X msg 700 320 1;
#X msg 1000 480 1;
#X obj 1000 540 s \$0-own;
#X text 700 620 The port goes into the cold inlet at load \, which is u_init's proven pattern rather than a creation argument on the object -- Pd 0.49 does not warn about extra creation arguments at all \, so a silent [midiout 1] would prove nothing either way., f 50;
#X text 20 580 ORDER MATTERS AND COMES FROM THE TRIGGER: Programmer Mode first \, off the right outlet \, then ownership. Raising ownership is what makes g_grid start painting \, so it must not happen while the device is still in a stock layout., f 58;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 3 1 4 0;
#X connect 3 0 8 0;
#X connect 4 0 5 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X connect 7 0 5 1;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X restore 20 600 pd init;
#N canvas 40 40 1200 900 safe-exit 0;
#X text 20 20 Returns the Launchpad to Live Mode. Entering Programmer Mode by SysEx locks out the device's own Settings menu \, so if Pd dies while it is in that state the only way back is a power cycle. This is the one message in Cut It that is worth more than everything around it., f 58;
#X text 20 140 quitting comes from mother.pd \, which sends it and then waits 100 ms before quitting Pd. That is the entire budget -- enough for a nine-byte SysEx \, not for anything clever. Pd 0.49 has no closebang \, so this is the only shutdown hook there is., f 58;
#X obj 20 280 r panic;
#X obj 20 330 t b b b;
#X obj 20 430 r quitting;
#X obj 20 480 t b b b;
#X msg 700 560 1;
#X msg 300 560 240 \, 0 \, 32 \, 41 \, 2 \, 14 \, 14 \, 0 \, 247;
#X obj 300 660 midiout;
#X text 20 700 THE GRID IS DELIBERATELY NOT CLEARED HERE. Live Mode hands the surface back to the device \, which redraws it \, and the 100 ms budget is better spent making sure the one message that matters gets out., f 58;
#X text 700 700 LIFTED FROM u_init WITH ONE PIECE LEFT BEHIND: the status panic footer. That is not the Launchpad's business -- u_init owns the footer \, and it keeps it. What moved is only the part that talks to this device \, which is the whole reason the lift happened., f 46;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 3 1 6 0;
#X connect 3 0 7 0;
#X connect 4 0 5 0;
#X connect 5 1 6 0;
#X connect 5 0 7 0;
#X connect 6 0 8 1;
#X connect 7 0 8 0;
#X msg 940 300 0;
#X obj 940 360 s \$0-own;
#X connect 3 2 11 0;
#X connect 5 2 11 0;
#X connect 11 0 12 0;
#X restore 20 700 pd safe-exit;
#X text 20 800 WHY THE SAFE EXIT LIVES HERE AND NOT IN u_init. Step 4 gives this file a layout table \, so it will be sending layout-select SysEx of its own. Two files sending layout messages to one device is the two-writers-one-surface failure the project bans everywhere else -- and the surface that would be lost is the one you cannot get back without a power cycle., f 58;
#X obj 1000 520 loadbang;
#X obj 1000 570 del 2000;
#X obj 1000 620 f \$1;
#X obj 1000 670 print m_launchpad-channel;
#X text 1000 730 Says which block it is listening on \, once \, a couple of seconds in. THE DELAY IS NOT COSMETIC: deploy.sh gates on OUTPUT rather than exit status and quits the check immediately after load \, so a print at loadbang would fail every deploy. Same treatment as m_nano's., f 50;
#N canvas 30 30 1900 1200 input 0;
#X text 20 20 EVERY BUTTON ON THE SURFACE. The 8x8 grid arrives as NOTES -- pitch r*10+c \, rows and columns both 1 to 8 \, row 1 at the bottom -- and the outer function ring as CC. Two decodes \, one emit chain \, and no notion anywhere of what any of them does., f 58;
#X text 700 20 THE GATE IS ONE CHANNEL \, not two. In Programmer Mode the Launchpad sends everything on its own channel 1 \, so the test is simply that the Pd channel EQUALS the block -- where m_nano needs a range because the nano's transport row sits on the channel above. Every other device in the rig is 16 or more away and cannot be mistaken for this one., f 58;
#X obj 20 300 notein;
#X obj 140 400 spigot;
#X obj 140 470 t b f;
#X obj 300 540 makefilename pad-%d;
#X obj 300 600 s \$0-name;
#X obj 140 600 f;
#X obj 420 360 - \$1;
#X obj 420 410 == 0;
#X obj 420 460 s \$0-lp-on;
#X obj 160 350 r \$0-lp-on;
#X obj 800 300 ctlin;
#X obj 900 400 spigot;
#X obj 900 470 t f b;
#X obj 1250 540 f;
#X obj 1250 600 makefilename lp-cc-%d;
#X obj 1250 660 s \$0-name;
#X obj 1550 360 - \$1;
#X obj 1550 410 == 0;
#X obj 1550 460 s \$0-lp-on;
#X obj 1040 350 r \$0-lp-on;
#X obj 20 780 list prepend;
#X obj 20 840 list trim;
#X obj 20 900 t a a;
#X obj 20 960 s disp;
#X obj 300 960 s param;
#X obj 300 720 r \$0-name;
#X text 20 1030 THE TWO DECODES RUN IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS \, and that is Pd's outlet order rather than a choice. notein fires channel \, then VELOCITY \, then PITCH -- so the pitch is what triggers an emit and the velocity is already stored. ctlin fires channel \, then controller \, then VALUE -- so there the value triggers and the NUMBER is stored. Hence t b f for pads and t f b for the ring: in both cases the name is built on the outlet that fires FIRST., f 58;
#X text 700 780 NAMES ARE PHYSICAL \, NEVER FUNCTIONAL -- pad-43 \, lp-cc-91. The pads are named for where they are \, which is the one thing about this surface that cannot change. THE RING IS NAMED BY RAW CC NUMBER ON PURPOSE: its numbering is documented but has never been confirmed on this unit \, and a generated name is honest about that where lp-top-1 would be a guess wearing a costume. Item 82 is what would let it become a position., f 58;
#X text 700 940 A RELEASE IS VELOCITY 0 \, so pad-43 0 follows every pad-43 127 and no note-off handling is needed. That also means a pad publishes twice per hit \, which is correct: what a press MEANS is u_map's business \, and some things want the release., f 58;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 2 1 7 1;
#X connect 2 2 8 0;
#X connect 4 1 5 0;
#X connect 4 0 7 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 7 0 22 0;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X connect 9 0 10 0;
#X connect 11 0 3 1;
#X connect 12 0 13 0;
#X connect 12 1 15 1;
#X connect 12 2 18 0;
#X connect 14 1 15 0;
#X connect 14 0 22 0;
#X connect 15 0 16 0;
#X connect 16 0 17 0;
#X connect 18 0 19 0;
#X connect 19 0 20 0;
#X connect 21 0 13 1;
#X connect 22 0 23 0;
#X connect 23 0 24 0;
#X connect 24 1 26 0;
#X connect 24 0 25 0;
#X connect 27 0 22 1;
#X obj 140 435 spigot;
#X obj 300 400 r \$0-own;
#X obj 900 435 spigot;
#X obj 1060 400 r \$0-own;
#X connect 3 0 31 0;
#X connect 31 0 4 0;
#X connect 13 0 33 0;
#X connect 33 0 14 0;
#X connect 32 0 31 1;
#X connect 34 0 33 1;
#X restore 20 1000 pd input;
#X text 250 1004 pads and the function ring -- named controls out on param and disp, f 50;
#N canvas 30 30 1600 1100 pressure 0;
#X text 20 20 POLYPHONIC AFTERTOUCH -- per pad \, simultaneous \, and the most expressive control on the rig. It must be enabled on the DEVICE and is not the default: hold Setup \, press the third Track Select button \, choose Polyphonic Aftertouch. The default is Channel Pressure \, one value for the whole surface \, which looks like a working decode reporting the wrong pad., f 58;
#X text 700 20 THE TRAP THIS FILE EXISTS TO AVOID. polytouchin emits the NOTE BEFORE THE VALUE \, so wiring it straight through lights a pad with the PREVIOUS event's pressure. Here the note goes to a COLD store and the value is what triggers -- the same shape ctlin needs \, for the same reason. It is written down in ref-midi.md because it has already cost time once., f 58;
#X obj 20 300 polytouchin;
#X obj 140 440 spigot;
#X obj 140 510 t f b;
#X obj 400 580 f;
#X obj 400 640 makefilename pad-%d-at;
#X obj 400 700 s \$0-at-name;
#X obj 700 360 - \$1;
#X obj 700 410 == 0;
#X obj 700 460 s \$0-lp-on;
#X obj 160 350 r \$0-lp-on;
#X obj 20 780 list prepend;
#X obj 20 840 list trim;
#X obj 20 900 s param;
#X obj 700 720 r \$0-at-name;
#X text 20 960 PARAM ONLY \, AND NEVER disp -- A DELIBERATE BREAK WITH m_nano. Every other m_ layer publishes to both off one t a a \, action first and report second. Pressure cannot: it is a continuous stream PER HELD PAD \, so a chord of four fingers would fill the OLED's five param rows and refuse everything else on the bus for as long as they were down. A control that can flood the display does not get to ask for it. What the pressure MEANS is still u_map's business \, and u_map listens on param., f 90;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 2 1 5 1;
#X connect 2 2 8 0;
#X connect 4 1 5 0;
#X connect 4 0 12 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X connect 9 0 10 0;
#X connect 11 0 3 1;
#X connect 12 0 13 0;
#X connect 13 0 14 0;
#X connect 15 0 12 1;
#X obj 140 475 spigot;
#X obj 300 440 r \$0-own;
#X connect 3 0 17 0;
#X connect 17 0 4 0;
#X connect 18 0 17 1;
#X restore 20 1100 pd pressure;
#X text 250 1104 polyphonic aftertouch -- param only, f 50;
#X obj 1400 900 r \$0-own;
#X obj 1400 960 outlet;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 7 0 8 0;
#X connect 16 0 17 0;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X connect 9 0 10 0;
